alwaysApply: false
---

Use the full-stack parent instructions first, then the frontend child instructions whenever the task involves frontend UI, styling, route composition, same-origin API routes, server workflows under `lib/server`, frontend tests, frontend docs, or backend integration from the UI.

For frontend feature requests, the parent instructions include a clarification-first workflow:

- ask 3 important questions when the request is ambiguous
- confirm whether the work is UI-only, Next.js task/API work, or both
- confirm route, interaction flow, and UI states before implementation
- confirm or recommend API inputs and outputs when data or task execution is involved
- prefer extending `app/api` + `lib/server` for TypeScript-native auth and task workflows instead of adding a Python pass-through by default
- keep App Router handlers thin and put validation, retries, caching, and provider calls in `lib/server`
- keep browser callers in `lib/api` and shared envelopes in `types`
- keep product auth cookies HttpOnly and verify JWTs server-side; never mirror session JWTs into `document.cookie`
- reuse an existing section, API helper, or server workflow pattern when relevant
- add or update tests under `frontend/tests/` for auth/session and server workflows
- keep the visual language consistent
- design pages like real product UI, not filler-heavy showcase screens
- treat the repo as a SaaS workspace by default rather than a marketing template
- keep copy brief and useful
- visually integrate third-party controls into the surrounding theme
- design flows before screens and verify state handling, recovery, and mobile fit
- keep domain rules out of presentation components and keep UI responsibilities local to the surface
- avoid decorative filler, nested cards, and generic hero layouts when building actual product pages
- keep auth and access pages especially lean: utility-first layout, one primary action, no promotional card grids unless explicitly requested
- default to safer frontend persistence: do not drop secrets into browser storage and be explicit about cache freshness for each new data surface
- keep provider and auth secrets server-only; never move `AUTH_SECRET_KEY` or `INTERFAZE_*` into `NEXT_PUBLIC_*`
- treat browser caching as a product decision with scenarios: `no-store` for auth and user-sensitive data, short-lived or focus-based revalidation for dashboards and search, and longer caching only for public reference content
- for public-facing SaaS pages, prefer a predictable structure with clear hero, proof, product demo, stepwise explanation, and repeated CTA rather than one-off layout experiments
- for protected app surfaces, default to a familiar top-nav SaaS shell, compact headers, light neutral surfaces, and practical page flow over showcase-style layouts
- keep selected navigation states on the theme accent with high-contrast text, usually white, instead of dark text on a saturated chip
- avoid persistent sidebars and heavy decorative chrome unless the workflow clearly benefits from them
- commit to a clear aesthetic direction and avoid generic AI-generated frontend patterns
- self-review hierarchy, spacing, states, and mobile fit before finishing
- verify with lint, typecheck, test, and build

For frontend documentation work, prefer Mermaid-first architecture docs with concise supporting text.

